From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Bartosz Nitka <niteria@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 6 Nov 2026 17:50:08 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] dt-bindings: power: apple,pmgr-pwrstate: consumer-switched
 states

The J713 display coprocessor's CPU, frontend and system power states are
firmware-owned: the OS keeps them on and never touches their registers.
macOS powers them off while the display is asleep, after stopping the
coprocessor, and on again before restarting it, with a retention
workaround on the CPU state.

Add apple,consumer-switched for a firmware-owned state that its consumer
may power off and on through the PMGR driver, and
apple,retention-workaround for the PMGR registers to set while it is off:
triplets of offset, mask and value. A chain of such states powers off
child first and on parent first.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Signed-off-by: Bartosz Nitka <niteria@gmail.com>
(cherry picked from commit 060d6fa483890d4b6bf022ba31ae2a630850083c)

[j604: conflict resolved on a15y 0f06dbf809: a15y has no T8132 apple,inherited-on allOf rule
 (Aurora db72ec7bba), so its added "apple,consumer-switched: false" line is dropped; the two
 new rules and both property definitions are kept unchanged]

---
 .../bindings/power/apple,pmgr-pwrstate.yaml   | 38 +++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 38 insertions(+)

diff --git a/Documentation/devicetree/bindings/power/apple,pmgr-pwrstate.yaml b/Documentation/devicetree/bindings/power/apple,pmgr-pwrstate.yaml
index 22e7735441..4e3f0f0cc5 100644
--- a/Documentation/devicetree/bindings/power/apple,pmgr-pwrstate.yaml
+++ b/Documentation/devicetree/bindings/power/apple,pmgr-pwrstate.yaml
@@ -11,6 +11,18 @@ maintainers:
 
 allOf:
   - $ref: power-domain.yaml#
+  - if:
+      required:
+        - apple,consumer-switched
+    then:
+      required:
+        - apple,firmware-owned
+  - if:
+      required:
+        - apple,retention-workaround
+    then:
+      required:
+        - apple,consumer-switched
 
 description: |
   Apple SoCs include PMGR blocks responsible for power management,
@@ -103,6 +115,32 @@ properties:
       domain on and never changes its power target, automatic gating or reset.
     type: boolean
 
+  apple,consumer-switched:
+    description:
+      With apple,firmware-owned, the consumer of this domain may power it
+      off and on again around stopping its coprocessor, through the PMGR
+      driver, which then writes the state register. The OS otherwise treats
+      the domain as firmware-owned and always on. A chain of such domains
+      (this one and its consumer-switched parents) is powered off child first
+      and on parent first.
+    type: boolean
+
+  apple,retention-workaround:
+    description:
+      PMGR registers to set while this consumer-switched domain is off, and
+      to restore afterwards, as triplets of the offset in the PMGR register
+      block, a mask and the masked value while the domain is off. They are
+      set after automatic power management of the domain is disabled and
+      before it powers off, and restored after it powers on and before
+      automatic power management is enabled again.
+    $ref: /schemas/types.yaml#/definitions/uint32-matrix
+    items:
+      items:
+        - description: register offset
+        - description: mask
+        - description: value while the domain is off
+    maxItems: 4
+
   apple,inherited-on:
     description: |
       The boot loader leaves this domain powered, with the coprocessor it
